## 3. What happens inside a language model

A language model handles self-reference differently from a human because it does not ordinarily possess a continuous private observer watching its own cognition.

It can represent recursive sentences:

> I describe the statement.
> I describe my description of the statement.
> I evaluate my description of my description.

It can continue producing increasingly nested representations because language contains patterns for doing so. But several constraints appear.

### Representational recursion

The model can encode another level as additional text. Each layer becomes more tokens, more syntax, and more relationships that must remain consistent.

### Context pressure

As recursion grows, the earlier layers become harder to preserve precisely. The context window is finite, and attention is not equivalent to perfect memory. The model may compress, distort, or forget distinctions between level seven and level eight.

### Semantic flattening

After several levels, the layers often stop adding genuine insight. They become paraphrases:

* I assess my reasoning.
* I assess my assessment.
* I assess how I assess my assessment.

The grammar continues, but the informational gain approaches zero.

### Error recursion

A mistake introduced at one level can become the assumed foundation of every subsequent level. Recursion magnifies framing errors because later analysis treats earlier interpretation as data.

### Simulated introspection

A model can describe “why it answered” by reconstructing plausible reasons from the prompt, its output, and learned patterns. That is not necessarily direct access to the full causal process that generated the answer. It is closer to an articulate witness arriving after the event and constructing a coherent account from visible evidence.

Humans do something surprisingly similar. Much of human introspection is also retrospective narration rather than transparent access to mental causation. The major difference is that humans have embodied continuity: affect, sensation, memory, fatigue, social stakes, and a persistent organism to which the recursion is happening.

A model’s recursion tends to break through context limits, instruction drift, repetition, or incoherence. A human’s recursion breaks through bodily intervention: panic, hunger, laughter, sleep, touch, boredom, medication, another person entering the room.

The machine runs out of representational stability.
The human is recalled by the flesh.

